§ `The questions of the employee bringing the claim`

§§ `Unfair dismissal (Chapter 2 Part 6)`
-- Situation: you have been dismissed, or threatened with dismissal.

@export Can I bring an unfair dismissal claim to the NSW Industrial Relations Commission (Industrial Relations Act 1996 ss 5, 83, 84)?
GIVEN `I was employed by the employer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were employed in any trade, business, project or occupation (on salary, wages, piece rates or commission, full-time, part-time or casual, including on a labour-only contract or as an outworker)
      `Schedule 1 deems me an employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are one of the persons Schedule 1 of the Act deems to be an employee (for example certain carriers, cleaners, timber workers or salespeople), if you were not otherwise employed
      `who engaged me` IS A `Who engaged me` @desc Who employed or engaged you: someone other than your spouse, de facto partner or parent; your spouse or de facto partner; or your parent
      `I am a public sector employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a NSW public sector employee (Public Service, NSW Health Service, Teaching Service, Transport Service, Parliament, or otherwise in the service of the Crown)
      `I am an apprentice or trainee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were an apprentice or trainee under the Apprenticeship and Traineeship Act 2001 who is not an existing worker trainee
      `I hold a senior office` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a Department Secretary or Public Service senior executive under the Government Sector Employment Act 2013, or a police executive officer under Part 5 of the Police Act 1990
      `a regulation exempts my class` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a regulation under the Act exempts your class of employee from the unfair dismissal provisions (such as some fixed-term, probationary or short-term casual employees)
      `I was employed under a Federal award` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were employed in NSW under a Federal award (a modern award under the Fair Work Act 2009, or a Division 2B State award)
      `I can apply to the Fair Work Commission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can apply to the Fair Work Commission about this dismissal on the ground that it was harsh, unjust or unreasonable, or could but for an exclusion such as a fixed term, probation or short casual engagement
      `my conditions were set by an industrial instrument` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your conditions of employment were set by an industrial instrument (an award, enterprise agreement, Federal award or other industrial instrument)
      `my annual remuneration` IS A NUMBER @desc Your annual remuneration, in dollars
      `a regulation prescribes a higher threshold` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you know of a regulation prescribing a higher income threshold for s 83(1)(b) than the Act's $62,200
      `the prescribed threshold` IS A NUMBER @desc That prescribed threshold, in dollars (any number if none)
      `what happened to me` IS A `What happened to me` @desc What happened: you were dismissed; you were threatened with dismissal; your services were dispensed with or your appointment annulled; or neither
      `I say it was harsh, unreasonable or unjust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim the dismissal (or threat) was harsh, unreasonable or unjust
GIVETH A `Part 6 answer`
`Can I bring an unfair dismissal claim` MEANS
    `the Part 6 answer` `I was employed by the employer` `Schedule 1 deems me an employee` `who engaged me` `I am a public sector employee` `I am an apprentice or trainee` `I hold a senior office` `a regulation exempts my class` `I was employed under a Federal award` `I can apply to the Fair Work Commission` `my conditions were set by an industrial instrument` `my annual remuneration` `a regulation prescribes a higher threshold` `the prescribed threshold` `what happened to me` `I say it was harsh, unreasonable or unjust`

@export By when must I apply to the NSW Industrial Relations Commission about my dismissal (Industrial Relations Act 1996 s 85(1))?
GIVEN `the day I was dismissed` IS A DATE @desc The day you were dismissed (or threatened with dismissal)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply about my dismissal` MEANS `the s 85 last day` `the day I was dismissed`

@export Am I, or was I, in time to apply about my dismissal, and can a late application still be accepted (Industrial Relations Act 1996 s 85)?
GIVEN `the day I was dismissed` IS A DATE @desc The day you were dismissed (or threatened with dismissal)
      `I have applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already made your application to the Commission
      `the day I applied` IS A DATE @desc The day you made it (any day if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `I made a similar Commonwealth application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you first made a similar application about the same dismissal under Commonwealth law (for example to the Fair Work Commission)
      `it was in time under Commonwealth law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that application was made within the time Commonwealth law required
      `it was settled or determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that application has been settled or determined
      `it was withdrawn or declined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that application was withdrawn, or declined because of the remedy available under the NSW Act
      `the day it was withdrawn or declined` IS A DATE @desc The day it was withdrawn or declined (any day otherwise)
GIVETH A `Part 6 time answer`
`Am I in time to apply about my dismissal` MEANS
    `the s 85 answer` `the day I was dismissed` `I have applied` `the day I applied` `today` `I made a similar Commonwealth application` `it was in time under Commonwealth law` `it was settled or determined` `it was withdrawn or declined` `the day it was withdrawn or declined`

@export What can the NSW Industrial Relations Commission order for my unfair dismissal, and what is the most I can be paid (Industrial Relations Act 1996 ss 89, 90)?
GIVEN `the Commission's view` IS A `Commission's view on reinstatement` @desc The Commission's view: reinstatement is practicable; reinstatement is impracticable but re-employment in another suitable position is practicable; or both are impracticable
      `another law gives me redress` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another Act or statutory instrument gives you redress for the dismissal (for example an anti-discrimination complaint)
      `I have started proceedings under it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have started proceedings under that other law
      `I lodged an undertaking not to proceed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have lodged a written undertaking not to proceed under that other law
      `my pay in the 6 months before dismissal` IS A NUMBER @desc Your remuneration in the 6 months immediately before you were dismissed, in dollars
      `the shortfall on leave without full pay` IS A NUMBER @desc If you were on leave without full pay in those 6 months, the extra you would have received on full pay, in dollars (0 if none)
      `the pay I lost until reinstatement` IS A NUMBER @desc The remuneration you would have received, but for the dismissal, until you are reinstated or re-employed, in dollars
GIVETH A `Part 6 remedy answer`
`What can the Commission order for my dismissal` MEANS
    `the Part 6 remedy` `the Commission's view` `another law gives me redress` `I have started proceedings under it` `I lodged an undertaking not to proceed` `my pay in the 6 months before dismissal` `the shortfall on leave without full pay` `the pay I lost until reinstatement`

§§ `Public sector disciplinary appeals (Chapter 2 Part 7)`
-- Situation: you are a NSW public sector employee and your employer has made a disciplinary or similar decision about you.

@export Can I appeal to the NSW Industrial Relations Commission against my public sector employer's decision (Industrial Relations Act 1996 ss 91, 92, 97, 98, 99)?
GIVEN `I am a public sector employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a NSW public sector employee (Public Service, NSW Health Service, Teaching Service, Transport Service, Parliament, or otherwise in the service of the Crown)
      `the decision` IS AN `Employer's decision` @desc The decision: to defer an increment (give the months deferred); to reduce your rank, classification, position, grade or pay; to fine you or forfeit pay; to annul your appointment on probation; to suspend you as a punishment; to dismiss you; to direct or require you to resign; or another decision
      `an instrument excludes my class` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an award or enterprise agreement, or an instrument or arrangement that barred appeals to the former Government and Related Employees Appeal Tribunal, excludes your class from appealing
      `I was engaged for a specified period or task` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were engaged under a contract for a specified period of time or a specific task
      `its length in months` IS A NUMBER @desc The length of that period or task, in months (any number otherwise)
      `I am serving probation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are serving a period of probation or a qualifying period
      `its length was fixed in advance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the length (or maximum length) of that probation was fixed in advance
      `the probation in months` IS A NUMBER @desc The length (or maximum length) of the probation, in months
      `the Commission finds it reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the probation is longer than 3 months: whether the Commission finds it reasonable, given the nature and circumstances of the employment and the probation rules that apply to you
      `I am casual for a short period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are engaged on a casual basis for a short period
      `I was regular and systematic for 6 months` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this employer engaged you on a regular and systematic basis for a sequence of periods over at least 6 months
      `I expected to continue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, but for the decision, you would have had a reasonable expectation of continuing employment with the employer
      `I elected to forgo the appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you elected in writing to forgo your right to appeal to the Commission against this decision
      `I went elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or someone for you) have appealed or brought other proceedings about this decision under another law, award or agreement
GIVETH A `Disciplinary appeal answer`
`Can I appeal against my employer's decision` MEANS
    `the Part 7 answer` `I am a public sector employee` `the decision` `an instrument excludes my class` `I was engaged for a specified period or task` `its length in months` `I am serving probation` `its length was fixed in advance` `the probation in months` `the Commission finds it reasonable` `I am casual for a short period` `I was regular and systematic for 6 months` `I expected to continue` `I elected to forgo the appeal` `I went elsewhere`

@export By when must I lodge my public sector disciplinary appeal (Industrial Relations Act 1996 s 100B(2))?
GIVEN `the day I was notified` IS A DATE @desc The day you were notified of the decision
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I lodge my disciplinary appeal` MEANS `the s 100B last day` `the day I was notified`

@export Am I, or was I, in time to lodge my public sector disciplinary appeal (Industrial Relations Act 1996 s 100B(2))?
GIVEN `the day I was notified` IS A DATE @desc The day you were notified of the decision
      `I have lodged it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have lodged your notice of appeal with the Industrial Registrar
      `the day I lodged it` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged it (any day if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Disciplinary appeal time answer`
`Am I in time to lodge my disciplinary appeal` MEANS
    `the s 100B answer` `the day I was notified` `I have lodged it` `the day I lodged it` `today`

§§ `Unfair contracts (Chapter 2 Part 9 Division 2)`
-- Situation: you do work under a contract or arrangement you say is unfair.

@export Is the contract under which I work an unfair contract (Industrial Relations Act 1996 s 105)?
GIVEN `it is an industrial instrument` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contract is itself an industrial instrument (an award, enterprise agreement or the like)
      `it is unfair, harsh or unconscionable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission finds the contract unfair, harsh or unconscionable
      `it is against the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission finds the contract against the public interest (including its effect on apprenticeship and training)
      `my total remuneration under it` IS A NUMBER @desc The total remuneration the contract provides for the work, in dollars
      `what an employee would receive` IS A NUMBER @desc What a person would receive as an employee performing the same work, in dollars
      `it avoids an industrial instrument` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contract is designed to avoid, or does avoid, the provisions of an industrial instrument
GIVETH AN `Unfair contract answer`
`Is my contract an unfair contract` MEANS
    `the s 105 answer` `it is an industrial instrument` `it is unfair, harsh or unconscionable` `it is against the public interest` `my total remuneration under it` `what an employee would receive` `it avoids an industrial instrument`

@export Can I apply to have the contract under which I work declared void or varied as unfair (Industrial Relations Act 1996 ss 105, 106, 108, 108A, 109A)?
GIVEN `my place in the contract` IS A `My place in the contract` @desc Whether you are a party to the contract, would be a party but for an order, or are not a party
      `it is an industrial instrument` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contract is itself an industrial instrument (an award, enterprise agreement or the like)
      `I perform work in an industry under it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a contract under which you perform work in any trade, business, project or occupation
      `it is related or collateral` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If not: whether it is a related condition or collateral arrangement to such a contract, and doing the work is a significant purpose of the arrangements
      `it is a contract of employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you do the work under it as an employee
      `my reason is one for unfair dismissal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the reason you say the employment contract is unfair is one for which you made, or could have made (or but for being excluded by s 83 could have made), an unfair dismissal application
      `my remuneration package` IS A NUMBER @desc Your remuneration package (pay, bonuses and employer-paid benefits such as superannuation and a private-use car) over the 12 months before you apply, or before the contract ended, in dollars
      `months it covers` IS A NUMBER @desc How many months of those 12 that package covers (12 if the whole year)
      `I am a partner and it is with my partners` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a partner in a business and the contract is between you and your partners
      `my profit share` IS A NUMBER @desc If so, your share of the net profits (and profit-based payments) over the 12 months before you apply, or before the contract ended, in dollars (0 otherwise)
      `a regulation prescribes a higher cap` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you know of a regulation prescribing a remuneration cap higher than the Act's $200,000
      `the prescribed cap` IS A NUMBER @desc That prescribed cap, in dollars (any number if none)
GIVETH AN `Unfair contract application answer`
`Can I apply about my unfair contract` MEANS
    `the unfair contract application answer` `my place in the contract` `it is an industrial instrument` `I perform work in an industry under it` `it is related or collateral` `it is a contract of employment` `my reason is one for unfair dismissal` `my remuneration package` `months it covers` `I am a partner and it is with my partners` `my profit share` `a regulation prescribes a higher cap` `the prescribed cap`

@export Am I, or was I, in time to apply about my unfair contract (Industrial Relations Act 1996 s 108B)?
GIVEN `the contract has ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contract has been terminated
      `the day it ended` IS A DATE @desc The day it was terminated (any day if it has not)
      `I have applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied to the Commission
      `the day I applied` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (any day if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH AN `Unfair contract time answer`
`Am I in time to apply about my unfair contract` MEANS
    `the s 108B answer` `the contract has ended` `the day it ended` `I have applied` `the day I applied` `today`

§§ `Victimisation (Chapter 5 Part 1)`
-- Situation: your employer, a prospective employer or a union acted against you.

@export Was I victimised by my employer or a union, so that the NSW Industrial Relations Commission can make orders for me (Industrial Relations Act 1996 ss 210, 213)?
GIVEN `I am an employee or job applicant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an employee, or a prospective employee, of the employer concerned
      `I suffered a detriment from their action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered a detriment as a result of action by the employer or an industrial organisation
      `the reason I allege` IS A `The reason alleged` @desc The reason you say they acted against you, from the list in s 210(1), or another reason
      `my activity interferes with my duties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the reason is public or political activity: whether that activity interferes with the performance of your duties
      `they showed it was not the cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer or organisation satisfied the Commission that the reason you allege was not a substantial and operative cause of what they did
GIVETH A `Victimisation answer`
`Was I victimised` MEANS
    `the s 210 answer` `I am an employee or job applicant` `I suffered a detriment from their action` `the reason I allege` `my activity interferes with my duties` `they showed it was not the cause`

@export By when must I apply to the NSW Industrial Relations Commission about victimisation (Industrial Relations Act 1996 s 213(3))?
GIVEN `the day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day of the victimisation (the contravention)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply about victimisation` MEANS `the s 213 last day` `the day it happened`

@export Am I, or was I, in time to apply about victimisation, and can a late application still be accepted (Industrial Relations Act 1996 s 213(3), (4))?
GIVEN `the day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day of the victimisation (the contravention)
      `I have applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied to the Commission
      `the day I applied` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (any day if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Victimisation time answer`
`Am I in time to apply about victimisation` MEANS
    `the s 213 answer` `the day it happened` `I have applied` `the day I applied` `today`

§§ `Unpaid pay and other money (Chapter 7 Part 2)`
-- Situation: your employer has not paid you money you are owed.

@export Can I apply to an industrial court (the Industrial Relations Commission in Court Session or an Industrial Magistrate) to recover unpaid pay, leave pay or superannuation from my employer, whatever my age (Industrial Relations Act 1996 ss 365-369, 377)?
GIVEN `the kind of money` IS A `Kind of amount` @desc What you are owed: an amount under an award, enterprise agreement or other industrial instrument (or long service leave or annual holiday pay); an amount under your employment contract where an instrument fixes the minimum rate for the work; such an amount where no instrument fixes it; pay for work whose rate no instrument fixes for you though one fixes it for others; or superannuation contributions an instrument requires
      `who applies` IS A `Who applies` @desc Who makes the application: you; an inspector, a Public Service agency employee or a union officer with your written consent; or someone applying without your written consent
      `the day it fell due` IS A DATE @desc The day the money became due
      `I have applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the application has already been made
      `the day I applied` IS A DATE @desc The day it was made (any day if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Recovery answer`
`Can I recover the money I am owed` MEANS
    `the recovery answer` `the kind of money` `who applies` `the day it fell due` `I have applied` `the day I applied` `today`

@export By when must I apply to an industrial court to recover money that fell due on a given day (Industrial Relations Act 1996 s 369(3))?
GIVEN `the day it fell due` IS A DATE @desc The day the money became due
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply to recover money` MEANS `the s 369 last day` `the day it fell due`

@export How much can an industrial court order my employer to pay me on a small claims application (Industrial Relations Act 1996 s 379(3))?
GIVEN `the amount I claim` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount you claim, in dollars
      `a regulation prescribes another maximum` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you know of a regulation prescribing a small claims maximum other than the Act's $10,000
      `the prescribed maximum` IS A NUMBER @desc That prescribed maximum, in dollars (any number if none)
GIVETH A `Small claims answer`
`How much can a small claims order give me` MEANS
    `the s 379 answer` `the amount I claim` `a regulation prescribes another maximum` `the prescribed maximum`
